Z||000000||KRMFiero||09-30-2001||08:28 PM||||Time has come to purchse another snowboard, I had a GNU Carbon High Beam 156, used it once and it fell of my friends jeep and got ran over, still was able to use it but i couldnt turn with it ?due to cracks?<P>anyways My Friend has a Burton Rippy 154 and i can use that good..... I need a board does anyone have any reccomendations for a size 154-156?  I have around 300$ to spend + some if i need to (parents)<BR>Also does anyone know of a cheap online snowboard store or a good used snowboard online store?<P>Kyle||||reg||4
Z||000001||Red2m4||10-01-2001||01:13 AM||||<BR>I have a "Function" board.  It's kind of a rare brand I think.  I like it though.<P>Generally, I've always been very happy with K2 products.  My friend has a morrow.  It's seems like a real good board.  If I were to buy a board to day, I'd probably get a morrow.  (Actually, I think Morrow is a k2 subsidiary.)<P>I bought my equipment used on consignment at a local ski shop.  It was "used" but looked brand new.  (199 for the board, 150 for k2 clicker bindings w/ boots.)<P>G'luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ferg||10-01-2001||09:12 AM||||I bought my first board, Rossignol, last year, (don't have specs on it). The board cost around $200 and with bindings and boots the final bill was $500 or so. I'm very happy with the construction and quality, plus the price wasn't too bad for a ski shop.<P>here's a pic- <A HREF="http://pages.prodigy.net/fergusor/misc%20stuff.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://pages.prodigy.net/fergusor/misc%20stuff.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||DrRodey||10-01-2001||01:51 PM||||Best two board companies out there are K2 and Division 23.  From there it really depends on what kind of riding you mainly do as to which model you should get.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||yo_sweet||10-02-2001||08:42 AM||||i have ahd the same snowboard for 8 years, its a kemper freestyle "back when kemper was kemper" flow bindings and no leash anyway..||||reg||1
Z||000005||Simple Fiero||10-02-2001||10:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DrRodey:<BR><B>Best two board companies out there are K2 and Division 23.  From there it really depends on what kind of riding you mainly do as to which model you should get.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Division 23(are they even still around) is okay, and K2 if you get into there high lines is awesome. But there are only 2 board companies which sit above them all and that is Burton(boots and bindings are f'in incredible).Plus Burton is suppose to have an incredible warranty. A friend of mine just busted his Craig Kelly Pro Model(now taht is old) and they told him that they couldn't replace it till he busted it in half. So he did just that and they sent him a brand new one(of course it wasn't the craig kelly,). plus there is Joyride, but I belive that they are built mainly for park, and Ride is also okay. Someone mentioned morrow they are excellent too. But if you want a great all around ride, look at libtech, very fast and light, hell if it wasn't for my heavy ass limited bindings, I could have launched off anything, man.<BR>Okay I have gave my .02. This topic is depressing me I have wrecked my ankle beyond repair and had to get rid of all my snowboard stuff. oh well more money for my fiero <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero_24||10-02-2001||01:19 PM||||I ride a K2 Zepplin, and I love it.  Burton is a good board as well.  I have a 1998 K2 zepplin that has never been ridden with bidings.  I might sell it.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||10
Z||000007||KRMFiero||10-02-2001||10:10 PM||||Hmm I think Im gonna go eather Burton or K2, went to snow board shop, they recomended i get the Burton Cruiser and use it as long as i can ($200 board) then after that board is "dead" get a better Burton...<P>U got mail<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000008||SilverFieroV6||10-03-2001||02:55 AM||||Just remember that with a burton board you have to have burton bindings and boots.<BR>Sam<P>------------------<BR>Gold 86 SE V6 4sp daily driver<BR>Silver 85 SE V6 auto ( actually running now)||||reg||1
Z||000009||Simple Fiero||10-03-2001||12:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SilverFieroV6:<BR><B>Just remember that with a burton board you have to have burton bindings and boots.<BR>Sam<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Like that is a bad thing lol. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||SilverFieroV6||10-04-2001||01:35 AM||||No its not really a bad thing just it can  be an expensive thing.  Right now i have a lamar board, Vans boots, and k2-step in bindings.  The board isnt the best board out there but for the way i ride its perfect.  Light and durable, I cant tell you haw many times that board should have broke from the way I have landed from tricks.  If I was getting a new board I would prob consider a k2 as my number one choice.<BR>Sam||||reg||1
Z||000011||Simple Fiero||10-04-2001||03:34 PM||||As much as I love burton, would definatly go with there boots and bindings, but I would probably go with a libtech board again, simply because Jamie Lynn is the greatest rider ever! What can I say i am old school. If it was new school I would go with Peter Line.||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroJoe||10-05-2001||11:56 PM||||I love my Ride Control board, have had it for 3 years and I've ridden it pretty hard.  No cracks and the base is holding up very well.  Its a 153, and I've grown considerably since I bought it so it's kinda small for me now but I can control it very well.  Its a directional, and I've done both park and alpine riding with it.  Burton Moto boots and Ride Preston LX traditional bindings.  Since I ride long trails or backwoods/moguls the most, the shortness of the board and traditional bindings makes it easier for big goon like me to be nimble in the trees  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Big Sky, Montana is the best place I have ever been, I want to go back this year.  The trails are very long and they have some really challenging double blacks.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Luni420||10-06-2001||01:58 AM||||I have a Liquid Man 162 with Lamar Bindings and Im quite happy. If I was to recommend a new board it would be a K2, Burton, or a Ride. You have to take into consideration if you want a trickboard, a mountainboard, or a halfy that does both relatively well. That will help you choose within the manufacturers product lines.||||reg||1
Z||000014||KRMFiero||10-07-2001||05:47 PM||||Well I went to the Friburg Fair in Maine this weekend, on the way back we stoped at a couple places in N.H. and i picked  up:<BR>Burton Frystyle Bindings - $100<BR>K2 (Model?, Lower line) 154 board - $250<BR>Healside boots (Upper line) - $150<P>Another few months and i get to try them out hehe, they look nice... Im not sure how good the board is, hopefully its alright for park and smaller mountins, cant wait<P>L8r<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
